Fixed in `tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:375`.

**Cause:** the permit counter packs the `CLOSED` flag into the low bit, above which the permit count is stored (`PERMIT_SHIFT = 1`). `forget_permits` read the whole word, shifted out the count, subtracted, and then CAS'd back `new << PERMIT_SHIFT` — reconstructing the word from the count alone and dropping the `CLOSED` bit. Any `forget_permits(n)` with `n > 0` on a closed semaphore silently reopened it: `is_closed()` went back to false and `try_acquire`/`acquire` started handing out permits again.

**Fix:** carry the flag bit through the CAS.

```rust
let new = curr.saturating_sub(n);
// Preserve the `CLOSED` flag stored below the permit count;
// forgetting permits must not reopen a closed semaphore.
let new_bits = (new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT) | (curr_bits & Self::CLOSED);
match self.permits.compare_exchange_weak(curr_bits, new_bits, AcqRel, Acquire) {
```

Since the CAS still compares against the full `curr_bits`, a concurrent `close()` between the load and the store makes the exchange fail and retry, picking up the flag on the next pass.

Verified: `forget_closed` fails on the unmodified tree and passes with the fix; the full `sync_semaphore`, `sync_semaphore_owned`, `sync_mutex`, `sync_rwlock` suites and the in-crate `semaphore_batch` unit tests all pass. Note `cargo fmt` isn't installed in this environment, so formatting is by hand — the line fits the 100-col limit.
